Q: Quotation marks at the start of all the lines of a paragraph? A: <p>That's indeed a good pointer. The only two missing details were the <code>box-decoration-break: clone;</code> and that it actually can work only on line (not block) boxes:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>blockquote {
  font-size: 20px;
  line-height: 1.25;
  padding-block: 1em;
  background-color: #fffdca;
  color: #6b644a;
  text-indent: 10px;
}

blockquote span {
  box-decoration-break: clone;
  background-image: radial-gradient(closest-side, currentcolor 0 70%, transparent 90%);
  background-repeat: no-repeat;
  background-position: .5em center;
  background-size: .3em .3em;
  padding-inline: 1em;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;span&gt;They have their Temples, whereof the chief, as the Jewish Temple did, stands in their metropolis; and is named Almack’s, a word of uncertain etymology. They worship principally by night; and have their Highpriests and Highpriestesses, who, however, do not continue for life. The rites, by some supposed to be of the Menadic sort, or perhaps with an Eleusinian or Cabiric character, are held strictly secret. Nor are Sacred Books wanting to the Sect; these they call Fashionable Novels: however, the Canon is not completed, and some are canonical and others not.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Sample adjustments: the blockquote content is wrapped in extra span (necessary, sadly) that is decorated with dots (for simplicity and independence from external resources).</p>

Q: Value Binding Leaves Property Null A: <p>Since you are a view model to do the saving. There is a much better way to do it.</p>
<pre><code>public class GenomeMatchingViewModel
{

    public Genome MatchBase { get; set; }    
    public MatchString MatchTarget { get; set; }
    Private readonly YourDbContext = new YourDbContext();

    public GenomeMatchingViewModel()
    {
        MatchBase = new MatchBase;
        MatchTarget = new MatchString();
    }

    public void SaveRecord()
    {
        db.MatchBase.Add(MatchBase);
        // do other codes
        db.SaveChanges
    }


}
</code></pre>
<p>Your controller should be like this</p>
<pre><code>public async Task&lt;IActionResult&gt; MatchCreation(int? id)
{
    if (id == null) return NotFound();

    var genome = await _context.Genomes.FindAsync(id)?? new Genome();
    GenomeMatchingViewModel matchVM = new GenomeMatchingViewModel();
    matchVM.MatchBase =  genome;

        //create a viewmodel here and pass to the view
    return View(matchVM);
}
</code></pre>
<p>The post action will be as simple as this</p>
<pre><code>[HttpPost]
[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]
//currently gives valid matchtarget and a null matchbase
public async Task&lt;IActionResult&gt; MatchCreation(Genome MatchBase,MatchString MatchTarget)
{

    GenomeMatchingViewModel matchVM = new GenomeMatchingViewModel();
    matchVM.MatchBase =  MatchBase;
    matchVM.MatchTarget = MatchTarget;

    //if (ModelState.IsValid)
    if (true)
    {
       matchVM.SaveRecord();
    }
    return View(matchVM);
}
</code></pre>
<p>You are already inserting a model to the view model so you do not to do binding. Just call the model name and map to the view model will do. Finally, it is recommending using dependency injection to do it,</p>
